  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV4">
<l>kūtir<anchor xml:id="kutir"/> vēṉil<anchor xml:id="venil"/> koḻuṅkā<anchor xml:id="kar"/> roḻipaṉi<anchor xml:id="pani"/></l>
<l>vēṉil muṉpaṉi<anchor xml:id="munpani"/> meyyuyar poḻutē.<anchor xml:id="polute"/></l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
   
<l>The cold season, the hot season, the season of heavy rain,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">Alternatively: ‘the season of full clouds’.</note> the season of ceasing dew,</l>
<l>the hot season,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">If one follows the order of the seasons presented in the commentary, then this refers to <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">iḷa vēṉil</hi></term> (‘young hot season’). It is rather confusing that in this <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>, the two hot seasons are not given different designations. This list of seasons may have been well-known enough for no specification to be necessary.</note> and the early dew season are the true<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mey</hi></q> literally means ‘truth’, 
  and I have taken it here to mean something like ‘in reality’. It is not entirely clear why its inclusion is necessary. To the best of my knowledge, <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">uyar</foreign> would make an acceptable <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cīr</foreign> on its own, so not for metrical reasons. A similar idea appears in TNV 6.3: <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">pōy tīr karu</hi></q> (‘constituent elements without falsehood’, i.e., ‘true constituent elements’). These phrases appear to function as fillers, without any particular semantic weight. If <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mey</hi></q> is included to mark an assertion made to counteract some perceived misinformation, then there is no evidence of what the ‘incorrect’ school thought.</note> 
  major times.</l></lg>
 </div>
 </body>
 </text>
 <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV4comm1">eṉpatu vain tiṇaikkuṅ kālamutal uṭ perum poḻutām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the way in which the great time-periods (i.e., seasons) occur amongst the basic time-elements.</p>
</div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV4comm2"> 
aippaciyuṅ kārttikaiyum ākiya kūtirk kālamum āṉiyum āṭiyum ākiya vēṉiṟ kālamum 
āvaṇiyum puraṭṭātiyum ākiya kār kālamumāciyum paṅkuṉiyum ākiya piṉ paṉik kālamuñ 
cittiraiyum vaikāciyum ākiya viḷa vēṉiṟ kālamumārkaḻiyun taiyum ākiya muṉ paṉik kālamumuṟaiyāṉē yain tiṇaikkum 
uyar kālamutal ām eṉṟa vāṟu.<anchor xml:id="varu"/> <num>4.</num></p>
<p xml:lang="en">The cold season comprising [the months] <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Aippaci</hi></term> 
and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Kārttikai</hi></term>; the hot season comprising [the months] 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Āṉi</hi></term> and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Āṭi</hi></term>; 
the rainy season comprising [the months] <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Āvaṇi</hi></term> and 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Puraṭṭāti</hi></term>; the late dew season<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">piṉ paṉi</hi></q> is the more common designation for <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">oḻi paṉi</hi></q> (‘the season of ceasing dew’) from the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>.</note> comprising [the months] 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Māci</hi></term> and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Paṅkuṉi</hi></term>; 
the young hot season comprising [the months] <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Cittirai</hi></term> and 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Vaikāci</hi></term>; and the early dew season comprising [the months] 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Mārkaḻi</hi></term> and <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">Tai</hi></term><note xml:lang="en" place="foot">In the Gregorian calendar, the cold season runs from mid-October to mid-December, the hot season from mid-June to mid-August, the rainy season from mid-August to mid-October, the late dew season from mid-February to mid-April, the young hot season from mid-April to mid-June, and the early dew season from mid-December to mid-February.</note> are the basic major time-elements of the five poetic settings<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">There are six seasons and five poetic settings. Purely based on the information provided so far in the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mūlam</hi></term> and the commentary, it is not clear how this difference is accounted for.</note> in order (i.e., respectively).
<note place="foot" xml:lang="en"><q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q> is a common term in commentarial 
literature and will appear elsewhere in this text. It means either ‘in accordance with tradition’ or ‘in order’. 
These get at similar things – the author is stating that the order of the listed items is the same as that of the 
aforementioned poetic settings (see TNV 3 and its commentary). Theoretically, it could also refer to a widely accepted 
enumeration given elsewhere, but since the order is the same as what we find in the preceding 
<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> and that is not always the case (see TP 16i, where 
the implied order is the same; see TP 6–12i, where the implied order is different), I think the
 author is referring back to their own list. Hence the translation ‘order’ rather than 
 ‘tradition’.</note></p>
 </div>
      </body></text>
